{"prdMarkdown":"# Product Requirements Document (PRD): Health Check Endpoint\n\n## Problem\nThe Ledger API currently lacks a dedicated health verification endpoint. Load balancers have no standardized mechanism to determine whether a service instance is operational and capable of serving traffic, which can lead to routing user requests to degraded or disconnected instances.\n\n## Goals\n- Provide an automated health check endpoint that verifies both web service responsiveness and underlying database connectivity.\n- Return HTTP 200 when the service and database are reachable, and HTTP 503 Service Unavailable when the database is unreachable.\n- Ensure load balancers can reliably drop unhealthy instances from active rotation.\n\n## Non-Goals\n- Exposing detailed service metrics, performance indicators, or resource usage stats (strictly binary up/down status).\n- Modifying or adding any data schemas or persistence models in `models.py`.\n- Implementing deep dependency checks beyond database connectivity.\n\n## User Impact\nEnd users of the Ledger API will experience increased reliability and fewer dropped or hanging transactions, as traffic will automatically be routed away from unhealthy instances during outages or maintenance.\n\n## Open Product Questions\n- What URL path (e.g., `/health` vs `/healthz`) and response payload format (e.g., plain text `\"OK\"` vs JSON `{\"status\": \"ok\"}`) should the endpoint standardize on?","trdMarkdown":"# Technical Requirements Document (TRD): Health Check Endpoint\n\n## Architecture Notes\n- Implement a lightweight route within `app.py`.\n- Use the existing `flask-sqlalchemy` setup (referencing `requirements.txt` and `models.py`) to run a low-overhead query (e.g., `SELECT 1`) to validate active database connectivity.\n- Ensure clean teardown and rollback of sessions so that frequent polling by the load balancer does not exhaust connection pools or leak handles.\n- Return an explicit HTTP 200 on query success, and intercept connection errors to return an HTTP 503 Service Unavailable response.\n\n## Edge Cases & Dependencies\n- **Unhandled Database Exceptions (`app.py`):** If the database is unreachable, a database driver error or SQLAlchemy exception (e.g., `OperationalError`) must be caught explicitly to prevent unhandled 500 crashes or worker failures.\n- **Connection Pool Exhaustion (`app.py`):** Frequent load balancer health probes can exhaust the SQLAlchemy connection pool if sessions are not properly closed or rolled back, especially on failed queries.\n- **Worker Thread Starvation (`app.py` / Gunicorn):** Running blocking database queries under slow network conditions or locked databases can saturate `gunicorn` worker processes. A strict query/socket timeout must be enforced.\n- **Thundering Herd / Cascading Outage:** If the database encounters transient latency or brief downtime, coupling the load balancer probe strictly to the DB could cause all instances to be simultaneously marked unhealthy.\n\n## Engineering Questions\n- What timeout duration should be configured for the health check query to avoid blocking Gunicorn workers during database latency?\n- Should we decouple a shallow liveness probe (verifying only process health) from a deep readiness probe (verifying DB connectivity) to prevent total service detachment during brief database blips?\n\n## Estimate\n- 2–3 days (up to 6 days worst case)."}